Electronics workbench, component knowledge and programmable devices (@ws-contract-electronics-workbench: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @workspace-contracts, #requires @ws-electronics-integration) { r_32BTZNYWD49F6RX79NBP814RW4
Owner refinement, 2026-11-22. Make electronics approachable to construct, inspect, experiment with and reuse as ordinary Workspace content. Reusable component/circuit knowledge supplies examples and models through providers. Generic editing, topology, measurement, simulation, firmware and domain coupling make those examples work. A circuit may have a realistic prototype view, a schematic, a fabricated-board representation or a cheap functional exterior with independent appearance and behavior policies.

[Development graph](../Workspace-Development.lingua) · [Contract catalogue](../Workspace-Contracts.lingua)

## Research and decisions

Research checked 2026-11-22. The original CRUMB is released; CRUMB Advanced currently lists Q1 2027 and publishes development documentation. Its claimed throughput and broad real-world parity are not measured evidence for Lince. Treat the published architecture and interactions as useful design evidence, with performance and validity requiring independent proof. [Original CRUMB](https://store.steampowered.com/app/2198800/CRUMB_Circuit_Simulator/) · [Advanced release listing](https://store.steampowered.com/app/5169160/CRUMB_Advanced/) · [Official development announcements](https://steamcommunity.com/app/2198800/announcements/)

CRUMB's component documentation combines a marked model, explicit connections and separate electrical/world behavior. Its breadboards use connected terminal groups, and wires can be edited after placement. Lince adopts data-defined parts and explicit physical connection operations through its existing model/recipe system. It retains typed ports and units instead of copying an untyped numerical mailbox or a particular scripting language. [Component architecture](https://crumbsim.com/components/) · [Building circuits](https://crumbsim.com/guide/building-circuits/)

CRUMB documents attaching instruments, circuit loading by measurement and fault inspection. Its motor example exchanges electrical and mechanical quantities at simulation boundaries, including integrated current for a slower physics step. Lince adopts source-linked instruments and explicit, independently validated multi-rate coupling. It does not assume one delayed exchange is stable for every mechanism. [Instruments](https://crumbsim.com/guide/instruments/) · [Motor example](https://crumbsim.com/components/example-motor/) · [Coupling](https://crumbsim.com/components/forces-and-coupling/)

Its sensing API distinguishes sampled environmental quantities from rendered camera images. Lince should let a sensor read an authored field, a geometric proxy or an implemented physical model, with provenance and independent detail. Changing viewport exposure cannot secretly change a calibrated lux input. [Sensing](https://crumbsim.com/components/sensing/)

Fritzing provides stable connectors across breadboard/schematic/PCB representations and internal buses for contact groups. CircuitJS packages selected circuits as reusable subcircuits with named ports and retained definitions. These suggest physical prototyping and Package as component over Lince's existing definition/occurrence/port model. [Fritzing part format](https://github.com/fritzing/fritzing-app/wiki/2.1-Part-file-format) · [CircuitJS subcircuits](https://www.falstad.com/circuit/doc/subcircuits.html)

KiCad separates part records, symbols, footprints and simulator models; it supports explicit simulator pin assignments, parameter tuning and saved analysis workbooks. Its library database does not supply a validated electrical model merely because it identifies a real part. Lince's catalogue must distinguish available assets, model/backend support and measured validity. Qucs-S documents backend incompatibility; Lince must expose and reject an unsupported solve or use an explicitly chosen approximation rather than silently opening/removing a component. [KiCad schematic/simulation manual](https://docs.kicad.org/10.0/en/eeschema/eeschema.html) · [Qucs-S backend behavior](https://qucs-s-help.readthedocs.io/en/latest/overview/interface-overview.html)

ngspice's XSPICE provides mixed analog/event-driven modeling, and its shared-library interface permits host coordination. These are evaluator candidates, with actual dialects, extensions, process isolation, scheduling and supported models pinned before selection. Reuse a trusted implementation; an adapter does not eliminate Lince's responsibility for clocks, coupling and source correspondence. [XSPICE](https://ngspice.sourceforge.io/xspice.html) · [Shared interface](https://ngspice.sourceforge.io/shared.html) · [Pinned version 47 manual](https://ngspice.sourceforge.io/docs/ngspice-47-manual.pdf)

Wokwi treats compiled firmware and chip configuration as inputs and offers custom device/protocol APIs, triggered logic capture and automated scenarios. Its custom-chip API is beta and automation is alpha; analog support is explicitly limited. Lince therefore distinguishes successful firmware execution, protocol correctness and electrical fidelity, and gives each a real proof. [Firmware configuration](https://docs.wokwi.com/vscode/project-config) · [Custom chips](https://docs.wokwi.com/chips-api/getting-started) · [Logic capture](https://docs.wokwi.com/parts/wokwi-logic-analyzer) · [Scenarios](https://docs.wokwi.com/wokwi-ci/automation-scenarios) · [Analog limits](https://docs.wokwi.com/chips-api/analog)

## Ownership and honest scheduling

The existing 11a and 15a plans already own pin mappings, schematic/PCB editing, DC/RC solving, model policy and stateful detailed/cheap transitions. Preserve those earlier usable exports. This contract adds:

| Package | Hard prerequisite | New owned capability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Electronic libraries](../features/Electronic-Libraries.lingua) | Schematics | Searchable, versioned part/circuit packages, source adapters, mapping/capability validation and offline closure. |
| [Physical prototyping](../features/Electronic-Prototyping.lingua) | Schematics | Breadboards, sockets, jumpers and explicit realized contact topology beside intended design. |
| [Instruments and experiments](../features/Electronics-Workbench.lingua) | Numerical runtime | Probe/loading semantics, triggered capture, fault diagnosis and user-authored test scenarios. |
| [Mixed-signal circuits](../features/Mixed-Signal-Circuits.lingua) | Numerical runtime | Actual digital/analog bridges, bounded event semantics, model adapters and imposed sensor inputs. |
| [Firmware devices](../features/Firmware-Devices.lingua) | Mixed-signal circuits | One real MCU/backend, compiled firmware, pin/peripheral execution and native debugging. |
| [Electromechanical devices](../features/Electromechanical-Devices.lingua) | Mixed-signal circuits and Rigid | One verified bidirectional electrical/mechanical device and load response. |

Libraries and physical prototyping can progress alongside PCB work after Schematics. Bench instruments and mixed-signal models can progress after the actual numerical runtime export; shared result/model/clock changes have one owner. Firmware then uses the mixed-signal export. A motor requires actual mechanics but no thermal solver. Independent adapters do not imply concurrent edits to a shared core.

[Electronics integration](../features/Electronics-Integration.lingua) owns late library/model, prototype/PCB and digital-capture/protocol adapters plus EB.W01–EB.W07 replay; providers retain their earlier bounded export.

This is a later expanded electronics acceptance join, not a new prerequisite for the existing core or base 15a acceptance. Each new package is planned work and closes only its own actual proof. Full EB acceptance additionally consumes 11a and 15a for PCB/device reuse and validated detailed/cheap transitions. Scientific authorization, measured limits and ordinary acceptance rules remain applicable. Core evidence must not claim these new capabilities before their providers pass.

## Requirements

- [ ] **EB.R01 — A component is an inspectable package.** Keep canonical component/terminal identities, source and local variants, logical role, symbol, connector/contact geometry, footprint/package, optional numerical/protocol model, firmware/storage assets and validation evidence independently versioned with explicit mappings. A manufacturer's part number, symbol name, package pin label and SPICE terminal order are different fields. Retain implementation internals when packaging selected circuitry as a component; expose selected parameters and ports without duplicating its source graph. Visual collapsing alone never changes execution.
- [ ] **EB.R02 — Knowledge through actual providers.** Implement a bounded local library, a real read-only catalogue connector and a supported part-file decoder with pinned import variants, typed quantity search, filters for asset/model capabilities, previews, attribution, revision pin/update/fork and offline closure. First prove the configurable SQLite catalogue over supplied part assets; database/service plugins share bounded discovery/query/fetch and source-revision semantics rather than requiring a particular vendor. Separate circuit recipes and test profiles from supplier catalogue facts, geometry assets and executable model support. Retain missing assets/models and unsupported dialects explicitly. Imported code remains inert until an admitted trusted evaluator runs it. A matching name or similar pin count never proves interchangeability. Source rights apply separately to symbols, geometry, model code/data and examples; downloadable is not synonymous with redistributable. [KiCad library terms](https://www.kicad.org/libraries/license/)
- [ ] **EB.R03 — Physical construction with explicit contacts.** Insertion, plug/unplug and supported solder/strip operations resolve stable contacts and commit topology through normal commands. Breadboard groups include rail breaks, socket occupancy and orientation. Wires carry endpoint identity separately from route shape and mechanical attachments. Preserve intended design versus realized connections, with source-linked opens/shorts and cross-probing. Mere visual intersection, proximity, shared color or a mechanical weld never creates a net. Model finite contact/wire impedance only when a supported model is selected; default ideal connectivity declares that assumption.
- [ ] **EB.R04 — Instruments are ordinary tools.** Attach probes to source identities in any supported view. Display units, reference/polarity, loading model, timebase, run revision and validity. Ideal observation and an electrically intrusive meter have distinct operations and evidence. Support differential measurements and separate references for isolated networks. Trigger/window/cursor behavior and bounded trace retention consume simulated time, independent of rendering. A selected trace reveals the originating component/net. Unknown or failed samples remain unavailable rather than displayed as zero.
- [ ] **EB.R05 — Time and performance are visible.** Distinguish electrical timestep, discrete event time, MCU cycle/instruction progress, mechanical step, instrument sampling, visual animation and wall time. Show achieved simulated-time/wall-time ratio and missed capture deadlines. Publish only settled accepted observations; event ordering, rollback and crossings obey the owning evaluator. Display decimation cannot silently hide an event promised to an assertion or trigger. A nominal MHz clock or fast current-dot animation is not a measured solver rate. Offscreen causal electronics retain admitted demand; a closed instrument releases only its own lease.
- [ ] **EB.R06 — Digital and analog have an explicit bridge.** Declare logic states including unknown/high impedance, driver resolution, open-drain/pull-up behavior, thresholds/hysteresis, references, output drive impedance, loading and timing. Contention, missing pull-up, invalid supply and out-of-range inputs produce specified statuses. Functional values, protocol messages and physically solved voltages are different interfaces. Choose a real bounded implementation and independently verify it; an arbitrary event queue cannot solve an algebraic electrical loop. Unsupported devices never silently disappear from the netlist.
- [ ] **EB.R07 — Programmable objects carry real firmware.** A device identifies MCU/backend/version, supported peripherals, firmware/toolchain hashes, memory map, pin functions, clocks, per-instance RAM/nonvolatile state and declared reset/power/snapshot behavior. Import a compatible compiled artifact and provide a native edit/build/load path using existing code editors and bounded jobs. Execute actual code through GPIO, ADC/PWM and UART plus one supported bus/peripheral. Each family/peripheral/timing or analog claim needs evidence. Firmware debugging does not certify silicon timing, RF, current consumption or analog physics. Device source import/open cannot flash hardware or start network/equipment actions.
- [ ] **EB.R08 — Component authoring shares native primitives.** Use the existing recipes, hierarchy, typed parameters and trusted electrical model adapters. Publish supported subcircuit/device parameter editing and connection mappings; validate the exact SPICE dialect/model family with real cases. Native custom-chip/protocol extensions require a separately admitted bounded runtime and explicit capabilities; arbitrary scripts do not become trusted because they are in a library. Built-in and user-created supported definitions use the same inspect/edit/package path. Immutable definitions, per-occurrence runtime state and saved configuration remain separate.
- [ ] **EB.R09 — The surrounding scene can supply a test environment.** Bind a device input to an explicitly chosen scalar field/region, geometric query, trace/stimulus or implemented physical model with units, frame, time, sampling and provenance. Begin with a bounded authored field and a known transfer law. Show imposed, estimated and physically solved inputs distinctly. Overlapping regions declare priority/composition and missing coverage. Viewport shading/exposure and purely decorative lights do not change quantitative sensor readings without an explicit calibrated coupling. Sensors, cameras and mechanical devices reuse existing scene/query/field services; no electronics-specific world is required.
- [ ] **EB.R10 — Feedback has a measurable domain contract.** Couple a supported electrical winding/driver to mechanical torque/load through typed SI ports, reference frames, sign conventions and settled observations. Specify sampling/integration/hold policy, energy accounting, timestep/convergence bounds and failure behavior. Preserve back-EMF/load response and avoid double counting power or torque. A motor animation can remain a cheap functional profile; a validated coupled profile has its own budget and range. Existing numerical transitions govern supported exchanges; arbitrary firmware/physical state cannot be reconstructed by toggling a detail slider.
- [ ] **EB.R11 — Explain faults without inventing physics.** Distinguish design-rule faults, incompatible/missing models, inconsistent equations/nonconvergence, exceeded ratings and an explicitly modeled damage state. Link diagnostics to source and relevant evidence; stop or preserve a marked last-valid run according to its policy. Parameterized threshold/dwell rules may support educational failures, with their imposed nature visible. Reset failure, power cycle, restart and checkpoint restore have declared effects. Generic 1 A limits or universal failure-open behavior are never assumed for all components.
- [ ] **EB.R12 — Circuits can carry repeatable experiments.** Capture source/model/firmware revisions, initial state, typed parameter overrides, stimulus schedule, observations, assertions/tolerances, seed where relevant, stop conditions and budget. Use normal controls to run, compare and inspect outcomes; native, Fiote and automated runners share accepted operations. First prove DC/transient tuning and finite sweeps, then digital/protocol scenarios at the join. Learning exercises and fault-finding challenges are reusable content over this interface. A successful nominal example is evidence for its stated range, not universal component validation. Trial edits/events stay isolated until explicit adoption.
- [ ] **EB.R13 — Validity is searchable and portable.** Show available modes and evidence per task: appearance/geometry, connectivity, functional behavior, firmware/protocol, analog transient, mechanical response and any specialist physics. Include model ranges, tested versions, uncertainty, numerical tolerances, known omissions, provenance and permitted dependencies. Library update invalidates affected evidence and derived models without replacing accepted designs silently. Save parts, examples, experiments and firmware through existing Workspace closure; offline use does not fetch dependencies, install evaluators or contact hardware. Keep configuration secrets outside shared packages.
- [ ] **EB.R14 — Fidelity and resources remain independent.** Reuse the five existing policy axes and 15a transitions. A detailed breadboard can use cheap logic; a simple box can run detailed firmware/circuits. Group strongly coupled electrical work, bound events/MCU instructions/traces/queues and avoid an independent solver per component. The earlier lamp/sensor transition proof remains valid; new model pairs require new output/loading/timing/state evidence. Pin workload/error/resource limits before implementation and benchmark numerical throughput separately from rendering. No automatic simplification may violate a retained observable or revive a second output writer.

## Native workflows and acceptance

### EB.W01 — Find, understand and package a part

Use ELB's real local/provider imports. Inspect a component's symbol, physical contacts and optional model mapping, detect a deliberately permuted model pin order, then pin/fork/update it and reopen offline. Package a selected supported subcircuit with explicit ports/parameters and an experiment. Insert two instances and verify shared definition versus independent runtime state. A metadata-only part stays useful without claiming simulation capability.

### EB.W02 — Build and reconcile a physical prototype

Use EPT's breadboard/jumper fixture with split rails, a deliberate off-by-one hole and crossing insulated wires. Construct through native insertion/wiring, inspect realized versus intended nets, repair the error, reshape a wire without changing connectivity, move/unplug and undo. Cross-probe schematic and prototype, then join 11a's PCB mapping through the same component identities. Opening a prototype does not require a fabricated board or active solver.

### EB.W03 — Measure, diagnose and compare

Run EWB's actual divider/RC fixture with nonloading and loading probes, trigger and cursors. Check analytic values and meter-induced changes. Diagnose open/short/reference/model/nonconvergence cases, pause on an imposed fault and distinguish reset operations. Run a user-authored parameter sweep with independent assertions, inspect the chosen candidate and adopt one change. Include short-pulse capture, bounded buffers, stale results and normal undo/offline reopening.

### EB.W04 — Build a mixed-signal reusable device

Run MSG's real logic/analog reference cases, including unknown/high-impedance states, open-drain pull-up, contention and threshold/hysteresis timing. Edit and package supported subcircuit parameters; import one compatible model and reject an incompatible one. Bind an authored environmental stimulus to a known sensor transfer law, move through its region and change viewport lighting. Only the declared stimulus/coupling changes the reading. Check limits, event ordering and current/loading separately.

### EB.W05 — Program a component and inspect its bus

Run FWD's actual compiled firmware fixture with timed inputs, ADC/PWM, UART and its chosen bus/peripheral. Capture digital events through EWB, implement triggered bounded logic/VCD capture and one actual protocol decoder for the chosen bus, and map a decoded transaction back to pins and firmware/run identity. Exercise wrong address, missing pull-up, unsupported peripheral, declared low-supply behavior, reset/power cycle and two independent instances. EBI owns this integration after both exports, coordinating shared instrument/result changes with their owners; the join cannot pass with only a promised decoder hook.

### EB.W06 — Close an electrical and mechanical loop

Run EMC's actual winding/motor/load reference, change mechanical load and stall the supported model, observe current/back-EMF/torque and compare independent equations. Measure multi-rate error and energy balance at the selected range. Replay 15a's existing cheaper-model transition on its validated fixture; declare new motor/firmware transition pairs separately. Keep an unrelated functional motor/light active and preserve its policy. Thermal/device-fabrication simulation is unnecessary for this bounded electromechanical claim.

### EB.W07 — Reuse and recover a complete electronic object

Save a reusable part/circuit with its symbols, contacts, supplied board mappings, model artifacts, firmware, experiments and permitted evidence. Reopen offline as ordinary Workspace data and inspect it through schematic/table/3D views with independent visual detail. Change a source/model during a run, cancel work, deny a dependency and observe from a restricted client. No stale activation, replayed external action, shared mutable per-instance memory or undisclosed private source appears. Check native focus and edit-panel-geometry alongside the existing core controls.

### EB.P01 — Independent correctness and usable controls

Recover each package's actual proof and pin a bounded combined fixture before the replay. Keep independent net/contact expectations, analytic DC/RC and loading references, logic truth/event sequences, compiled firmware output expectations and electromechanical balances. Do not validate a model solely against a second export of itself. Record native actions, observed results and diagnostics, unchanged earlier workflows, exact variants and unresolved limits. EB.W01–EB.W07 must be mapped to the applicable EB.R01–EB.R14 and provider tasks in the handoff.

### EB.P02 — Measured cost, limits and cleanup

Record component/net/contact counts, active solve groups, events/steps/instructions, buffers/sample retention, cold/warm setup, simulated-time/wall-time ratio, native latency and peak CPU/RAM/GPU/output costs on a named device. Exercise two views, long offscreen causal activity, cap reached, cancellation and last-demand release. Numerical tolerances/observables remain fixed during performance comparison. Large stored libraries or hidden circuit interiors cannot imply active evaluators. Share actual format/backend support and bounded measured results.

## Further methods and completion

Live audio is a promising later stimulus/output profile, illustrated by CRUMB's documented audio workflow. First qualify a bounded offline signal/filter method, then actual live capture/output with sample-rate conversion, latency/overrun and pause semantics through the existing media boundary. FFT, AC/noise analysis, additional MCU architectures, HDL and specialist RF/parasitic models have separately owned method proofs. Full RF propagation, temperature-dependent damage, physical equipment flashing and an obligatory new IDE are not consequences of the component schema. [CRUMB audio](https://crumbsim.com/guide/audio/)

These plans implement generic capabilities and open format/source adapters. There is no required CRUMB-specific integration or dependency on its component collection. Current outcome is unbuilt and unverified. Preserve the research, source links, exact supported methods, native evidence and unfinished obligations in the eventual handoff.
